          "type": "text",
          "text": "All operative source has been read (signer.py fully, serializer.py fully). I can now answer.\n\nSummary of findings (source explanation, not a proof — no runtime check was run):\n\n1. **Signing key position: last.** `Signer.derive_key()` defaults to `self.secret_keys[-1]` and `get_signature()` calls `derive_key()` with no argument; the `secret_key` property documents itself as \"The newest (last) entry\". The constructor doc and attribute comment state the list is \"oldest to newest\" and \"The newest (last) key is used for signing.\" `Serializer.dumps` signs via `make_signer(salt).sign(payload)`, and `make_signer` passes the whole ordered `secret_keys` list to the default `Signer`, so rotation behavior carries through unchanged.\n\n2. **Primary signer verification key order: newest-first.** `Signer.verify_signature` loops `for secret_key in reversed(self.secret_keys)`, and since the list is stored oldest→newest, `reversed` tries newest first.\n\n3. **Fallback signer key order: oldest-first.** In `Serializer.iter_unsigners`, each fallback signer config is expanded with `for secret_key in self.secret_keys: yield fallback(secret_key, ...)` — plain list order, i.e., oldest to newest, one single-key Signer instance per key.\n\n4. **Primary before fallback: yes.** `iter_unsigners` yields `self.make_signer(salt)` first, then the fallbacks; `loads` iterates that generator in order. Note the default `default_fallback_signers` is `[]`, so fallbacks only run when configured, but when present they always come after the primary signer and its own newest-first key loop. Helper `_make_keys_list` preserves the caller's input order.\n\n{\"action\":\"finish\",\"answer\":{\"signing_key_position\":\"last\",\"primary_verification_key_order\":\"newest-first\",\"fallback_key_order\":\"oldest-first\",\"primary_before_fallback\":true,\"claims\":[{\"claim\":\"The newest (last) key in the supplied oldest-to-newest list is used to create new signatures; Signer.derive_key defaults to the last item and get_signature/sign use that default. The Serializer's dumps signs through make_signer, which forwards the full ordered key list to the default Signer.\",\"citations\":[{\"path\":\"src/itsdangerous/signer.py\",\"quote\":\":param secret_key: The secret key to sign and verify with. Can be a\\n        list of keys, oldest to newest, to support key rotation.\"},{\"path\":\"src/itsdangerous/signer.py\",\"quote\":\"#: The list of secret keys to try for verifying signatures, from\\n        #: oldest to newest. The newest (last) key is used for signing.\"},{\"path\":\"src/itsdangerous/signer.py\",\"quote\":\"@property\\n    def secret_key(self) -> bytes:\\n        \\\"\\\"\\\"The newest (last) entry in the :attr:`secret_keys` list. This\\n        is for compatibility from before key rotation support was added.\\n        \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n        return self.secret_keys[-1]\"},{\"path\":\"src/itsdangerous/signer.py\",\"quote\":\"if secret_key is None:\\n            secret_key = self.secret_keys[-1]\"},{\"path\":\"src/itsdangerous/signer.py\",\"quote\":\"def get_signature(self, value: str | bytes) -> bytes:\\n        \\\"\\\"\\\"Returns the signature for the given value.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n        value = want_bytes(value)\\n        key = self.derive_key()\"},{\"path\":\"src/itsdangerous/serializer.py\",\"quote\":\"rv = self.make_signer(salt).sign(payload)\"},{\"path\":\"src/itsdangerous/serializer.py\",\"quote\":\"return self.signer(self.secret_keys, salt=salt, **self.signer_kwargs)\"}]},{\"claim\":\"The primary (default) signer verifies candidate keys in reversed list order, i.e., newest-first, returning True on the first matching key.\",\"citations\":[{\"path\":\"src/itsdangerous/signer.py\",\"quote\":\"for secret_key in reversed(self.secret_keys):\\n            key = self.derive_key(secret_key)\\n\\n            if self.algorithm.verify_signature(key, value, sig):\\n                return True\\n\\n        return False\"}]},{\"claim\":\"Individual keys are passed to fallback signers in plain list order (oldest to newest), one single-key Signer instance per key per fallback configuration. _make_keys_list preserves the caller's input order.\",\"citations\":[{\"path\":\"src/itsdangerous/serializer.py\",\"quote\":\"for fallback in self.fallback_signers:\\n            if isinstance(fallback, dict):\\n                kwargs = fallback\\n                fallback = self.signer\\n            elif isinstance(fallback, tuple):\\n                fallback, kwargs = fallback\\n            else:\\n                kwargs = self.signer_kwargs\\n\\n            for secret_key in self.secret_keys:\\n                yield fallback(secret_key, salt=salt, **kwargs)\"},{\"path\":\"src/itsdangerous/signer.py\",\"quote\":\"return [want_bytes(s) for s in secret_key]  # pyright: ignore\"}]},{\"claim\":\"The primary signer runs before the fallback signers: iter_unsigners yields the configured signer first, then each fallback, and loads tries them in that generator order. (With default Serializer settings default_fallback_signers is empty, so fallbacks only run when explicitly configured.)\",\"citations\":[{\"path\":\"src/itsdangerous/serializer.py\",\"quote\":\"\\\"\\\"\\\"Iterates over all signers to be tried for unsigning. Starts\\n        with the configured signer, then constructs each signer\\n        specified in ``fallback_signers``.\\n        \\\"\\\"\\\"\"},{\"path\":\"src/itsdangerous/serializer.py\",\"quote\":\"yield self.make_signer(salt)\\n\\n        for fallback in self.fallback_signers:\"},{\"path\":\"src/itsdangerous/serializer.py\",\"quote\":\"for signer in self.iter_unsigners(salt):\\n            try:\\n                return self.load_payload(signer.unsign(s))\\n            except BadSignature as err:\\n                last_exception = err\"},{\"path\":\"src/itsdangerous/serializer.py\",\"quote\":\":param fallback_signers: List of signer parameters to try when\\n        unsigning with the default signer fails.\"}]}],\"uncertainties\":\"This is a static source reading of itsdangerous (Signer/Serializer defaults assumed), not a formal proof or runtime test; behavior was not executed. Quotes were taken from full file reads of src/itsdangerous/signer.py and src/itsdangerous/serializer.py. Default fallback_signers is an empty list, so the fallback ordering claims apply when fallback signers are supplied.\"}}",
          